{"name":"Bleeding Heart","hex":"#c02e4c","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bleeding heart","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of various perennial plants, of the family Papaveraceae, subfamily Fumarioideae, often having pink heart-shaped flowers, including:\nAn Asian bleeding heart (Lamprocapnos spectabilis, syn. Dicentra spectabilis), a popular garden plant with arching sprays of pendent red and white (or pure white) flowers.\nAny member of the genus Dicentra (bleedinghearts), native to eastern Asia and North America, some species with colors and shape similar to Lamprocapnos spectabilis.\nDactylicapnos, a genus native to Himalayas and mountainous areas of South China, Burma and Vietnam, usually with yellow flowers of similar shape.","An Asian bleeding heart (Lamprocapnos spectabilis, syn. Dicentra spectabilis), a popular garden plant with arching sprays of pendent red and white (or pure white) flowers."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bleeding_heart"}],"entries":[{"title":"Papaveraceae","blocks":[{"text":"The Papaveraceae, informally called the poppy family, are an economically important family of about 42 genera and approximately 775 known species of flowering plants in the order Ranunculales. The family is cosmopolitan, occurring in temperate and subtropical climates like Eastern Asia as well as California in North America. It is almost unknown in the tropics. Most are herbaceous plants, but a few are shrubs and small trees. The family currently includes two groups that have been considered to be separate families: Fumariaceae and Pteridophyllaceae. Papaver is the classical name for poppy in Latin."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/2010-06-29_%2828%29_Papaver_rhoeas.jpg/330px-2010-06-29_%2828%29_Papaver_rhoeas.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Papaveraceae"},{"title":"Lamprocapnos","blocks":[{"text":"Lamprocapnos spectabilis, commonly known as bleeding heart or Asian bleeding heart, is a species of flowering plant belonging to the fumitory subfamily (Fumarioideae) of the Papaveraceae. It is native to Northeast China and the Korean Peninsula; however, it has been introduced by humans into a larger area of Northeast Asia, including parts of Siberia, Russia and Japan."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Dicentra-spectabilis.jpg/330px-Dicentra-spectabilis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lamprocapnos"},{"title":"Bleeding Heart (album)","blocks":[{"text":"Bleeding Heart is one of several names given to albums of a 1968 jam session with Jimi Hendrix, Jim Morrison, and others. The albums were fashioned from an informal two-track tape recording made by Hendrix which was subsequently stolen from his apartment. The jam took place at the Scene, a nightclub in New York City, and various dates and participants have been suggested. Although it presents a unique setting, critics and biographers have generally found fault with the sound quality and Morrison's performance."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/8/87/Bleeding_Heart_%28album%29_cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bleeding_Heart_(album)"},{"title":"Bleeding","blocks":[{"text":"Bleeding, hemorrhage, haemorrhage or blood loss, is blood escaping from the circulatory system from damaged blood vessels. Bleeding can occur internally, or externally either through a natural opening such as the mouth, nose, ear, urethra, genitalia or anus, or through a puncture in the skin.\nHypovolemia is a massive decrease in blood volume, and death by excessive loss of blood is referred to as exsanguination. Typically, a healthy person can endure a loss of 10–15% of the total blood volume without serious medical difficulties. The stopping or controlling of bleeding is called hemostasis and is an important part of both first aid and surgery."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Bleeding_finger.jpg/330px-Bleeding_finger.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bleeding"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/HKKk9.jpg/500px-HKKk9.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/HKKk9.jpg/1280px-HKKk9.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/HKKk9.jpg/500px-HKKk9.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Horned poppy (Glaucium sp.)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHKKk9.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/UK_20230418_114354.jpg/500px-UK_20230418_114354.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/UK_20230418_114354.jpg/1280px-UK_20230418_114354.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/UK_20230418_114354.jpg/500px-UK_20230418_114354.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Roemeria sp.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUK_20230418_114354.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Hypecoum_procumbens_FlowerCloseup_25April2009_CampodeCalatrava.jpg/120px-Hypecoum_procumbens_FlowerCloseup_25April2009_CampodeCalatrava.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Hypecoum_procumbens_FlowerCloseup_25April2009_CampodeCalatrava.j